.Dd October 31, 2026
.Dt APREFETCH 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aprefetch
.Nd record boot-time file reads and prefetch them on the next boot
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "pseudo-device aprefetch"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver records which pages of which files are read while the system boots.
On the next boot it reads those pages ahead, in the order of their blocks
on the disk, so that one sweep of the disk replaces the seeks of a cold
boot.
Only regular files on FFS file systems are recorded and prefetched.
.Pp
A failure anywhere only means that nothing is prefetched; the contents of
files are never affected, and the driver never writes to a disk.
.Ss Recording
While a recording window is open, every read of a page of a file, whether
by
.Xr read 2
or by a page fault on a mapping, adds an access entry.
Reads served from memory are recorded too, so that the entries describe
everything the boot needed.
Consecutive reads of the same file by the same process are merged into one
entry.
Each CPU keeps its own ring of entries; when a ring is full, new entries are
dropped and counted.
.Pp
The boot window opens when the root file system is mounted and closes at
the first login
.Pq Dv APREFIOC_BOOTDONE ,
on
.Dv APREFIOC_RECSTOP ,
or after
.Va kern.aprefetch.record_timeout
seconds.
.Pp
.Xr read 2
returns
.Vt struct aprefetch_rec
entries, as defined in
.In sys/aprefetch.h .
It waits while the window is open and no entry is available, and returns
end-of-file once the window has closed and every entry has been read.
.Ss Replay
After the root file system is mounted, a kernel thread reads the boot
profile
.Pa /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof .
The profile is ignored, and only recording takes place, if it is missing,
not owned by root, writable by group or others, larger than 16 MB, or if
its magic number, version or checksum is wrong.
.Pp
The entries are in the order in which the data was first read; those that
fit in
.Va kern.aprefetch.boot_budget
are taken in that order and the rest is dropped.
Each file is looked up by its inode and generation number, so that an entry
for a file that was removed or replaced is dropped, as is the part of an
entry beyond the end of a file that has shrunk.
Entries for a file system that is not mounted yet are kept until it is
mounted.
The ranges are then mapped to disk blocks, sorted by disk and block number,
and read ahead asynchronously in that order, with at most
.Va kern.aprefetch.max_io
reads in flight.
.Pp
A page that a process is reading already is skipped.
A process that wants a page the replay has issued waits for that read; if
the read fails, the page is dropped, the process reads it itself, and the
replay does not read it again.
I/O errors are only counted.
.Pp
The budget is charged when a read is issued, not when it completes.
The replay stops when everything has been issued, when the budget is used
up, after
.Va kern.aprefetch.replay_timeout
seconds, when free memory falls below twice the pagedaemon target, or when
.Va kern.aprefetch.enable
is cleared.
However it stops, it waits for its reads to complete before it releases
the files.
.Ss Unmounting
Before an unmount suspends a file system, the replay drops the entries it
has not processed there, waits for its reads on that file system, releases
its files and only then lets the unmount continue.
The replay therefore never makes an unmount fail or wait for long.
.Ss Marks
A
.Xr write 2
to the device stores a mark: the label written, up to 47 characters
without a trailing newline, the time since boot, and the read counters of
the disk that holds the root file system.
.Pa /etc/rc
writes a mark before and after each
.Xr rc.d 8
script.
The kernel adds
.Dq kernel: root mounted ,
.Dq kernel: init ,
.Dq replay: start ,
.Dq replay: end
and
.Dq boot-done .
Marks are kept even when recording and replay are turned off, so that
a boot without prefetch can be measured the same way.
Up to 1024 marks are kept.
.Ss Turning it off
.Va kern.aprefetch.enable
turns recording and replay off.
On x86, booting with the
.Fl 4
flag of
.Xr boot 8 ,
which the default boot menu offers as
.Dq Boot without prefetch ,
turns them off for that boot.
.Sh IOCTLS
The following
.Xr ioctl 2
commands are defined in
.In sys/aprefetch.h :
.Bl -tag -width APREFIOC_GETMARKS
.It Dv APREFIOC_STATUS Fa "struct aprefetch_status"
Whether a window is open, since when, and whether a replay is running.
.It Dv APREFIOC_RECSTART
Open a recording window.
Entries of an earlier window that were not read are discarded.
.It Dv APREFIOC_RECSTOP
Close the recording window.
.It Dv APREFIOC_BOOTDONE
Close the boot window, if it is open.
.It Dv APREFIOC_FLUSH
Make the entries being merged available to
.Xr read 2 .
.It Dv APREFIOC_GETMARKS Fa "struct aprefetch_marks"
Copy the marks.
.It Dv APREFIOC_BMAP Fa "struct aprefetch_bmap"
Map a recorded range of a file to disk blocks.
.It Dv APREFIOC_REPLAY Fa "struct aprefetch_replay"
Replay a profile image now, with the given budget or
.Va kern.aprefetch.app_budget .
Fails with
.Er EBUSY
if a replay is running.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width kern.aprefetch.replay_timeout
.It Va kern.aprefetch.enable
Record and replay file reads; 1 by default.
.It Va kern.aprefetch.boot_budget
Bytes the boot replay may read; 256 MB by default.
.It Va kern.aprefetch.app_budget
Bytes a replay started with
.Dv APREFIOC_REPLAY
may read by default; 64 MB by default.
.It Va kern.aprefetch.max_io
Reads a replay keeps in flight, from 1 to 64; 8 by default.
.It Va kern.aprefetch.replay_timeout
Seconds after which a replay stops; 60 by default.
.It Va kern.aprefetch.record_timeout
Seconds after which a recording window closes; 120 by default.
.It Va kern.aprefetch.stats
Read-only
.Vt struct aprefetch_stats :
entries recorded and dropped, reads served from memory or not, and for the
replays the entries taken, dropped as stale and deferred, the reads and
pages issued, the pages that were in memory already, the pages lost to
errors, how the last replay ended and how long it took.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof -compact
.It Pa /dev/aprefetch
the device, readable and writable by root only
.It Pa /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof
the boot profile
.It Pa /var/db/aprefetch
the traces from which
.Xr aprefd 8
builds the profile
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aprefctl 8 ,
.Xr aprefd 8 ,
.Xr boot 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver was written for AutumnBSD.
.Sh CAVEATS
File system metadata, such as inodes and directories, is neither recorded
nor prefetched.
.Pp
The profile and the traces record which files were read and when.
They are readable by root only, and record inode numbers, not file names.
